From a79d84a4cd89a57fe42934dfd1ba13c3619ebd7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:47:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: what the kernel says of the converter behind
 the port is read after each look at the display, not with each picture

COutputHandover::ConverterVerdict() read DVBRIDGE_DV_CONVERTER from the
kernel at each call (drmModeObjectGetProperties(): two ioctls and the
arrays of every property of the connector), and DriverOf() did the same
for NV_DUAL_MODE_TMDS of nvidia-drm. Both are asked with each picture of a
Dolby Vision video (CWinSystemGbm::SupportsNativeDVOutput(), from
CLinuxRendererGLES::ConfigChanged(); its line of status asks the verdict
once more): two to four reads of the kernel for each picture on a
DisplayPort connector of i915 or xe that can carry the tunnel through the
converter of its board, or of nvidia-drm.

The kernel writes them only when it looks at the display: i915 and xe in
intel_dp_set_edid() and intel_dp_unset_edid() of the detect of the
connector (linux-9977), nvidia-drm in its detect
(nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9011). It runs that detect when a display is
plugged or unplugged, and then sends the hot-plug event, and when a
program probes the connector. CDetectedProperty keeps what was read, in
one atomic word with the count of those looks, and reads the kernel again
only after one: the hot-plug event of the device in use
(CDRMDisplayWatch), the output taken, the start of each video, and Kodi's
own probes of the connector (the look at the display of a video, the look
at the modes after an event). A value read before a look is never given
after it, from any thread; a read that fails is not kept.

A host test counts the reads (none for the pictures between two looks,
one after each), the change of the verdict after a look, another
connector, a read that fails, and threads that ask while the display is
looked at (ASan, UBSan and TSan); two mutants (no look counted, nothing
kept) fail it. A check of the source says that the handover reads both
through it and that every look says so.
